.TH WB\-START 1 "" "watchbill"
.SH NAME
wb\-start \- start a session of one role
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwb\fR start <role> [<issue>] [\-\-runner <name>] [\-\-prompt] [\-\-ignore\-local] [\-\-ignore\-kept] [\-\-ignore\-branch] [\-\-no\-push] [\-q | \-\-quiet] [the options of prompt]
.br
or <role> <issue> \-\-take\-back
.br
or \-\-list [\-v | \-\-verbose]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Start a session of one role, planner, tester, build, review or fix: the role is the word after start. The issue is needed by all but the review and the fix, which may be given \-\-change and no issue. It checks what prompt checks, that a fix follows a review, that no classed code is in its way (classed: of watchbill.yml), that the runner has start in the organisation's file, and that standard input and output are a terminal; then write the stamp of an issue as a commit on refs/watchbill/<issue>, signed by a key of .watchbill/signers with start (refused where the default branch has no list, or one with no key line), and push it to origin with no force: a push refused as not a fast\-forward ends with 1 and starts nothing, and with no origin the stamp stays local, said. Its copy is posted by issue_comment of watchbill.yml where it is there (a review or a fix of a change with no issue posts the stamp by change_comment), and the runner's start is run in the root of the checkout.
.PP
The runner is \-\-runner, or the organisation's default. The round is worked out from the stamp commits on the job's ref (from change_comments for a change with no issue), the change from change_of_branch; \-\-round and \-\-change win. Where the job's ref is not as wb writes it (one line of commits, with no merge, each signed by a key of .watchbill/signers with the role it needs), nothing on it counts, and the start, as prompt, ends with 1 before anything is written, naming the first commit that is not. \-\-prompt, \-\-stamp and \-\-parts print as prompt does, and post and start nothing. A view of the issue kept with work not taken back, from any role, stops the start with 1 before anything is posted; \-\-ignore\-kept goes on without it, and the stamp says so. The commits of the session come back onto the branch of the stamp, made where it is not there. Where it is there and HEAD is not its commit, the start ends with 1 before anything is posted. A checkout on another branch, but the default branch before the branch of the stamp is there, stops the start with 1 before anything is posted, and \-\-ignore\-branch goes on. After the tester, the build or the fix of an issue whose commits all came back, and whose session ended with 0, it pushes the branch to origin with git, never with force, and after a build with no change open it opens one as a draft by draft_change, with the description the session wrote; without change_of_branch and draft_change, or with \-\-no\-push, it says the commands and runs neither. \-\-take\-back, given with the role and the issue alone, starts and posts nothing: it says what it will take, then takes back the commits of the newest view of that role and issue that was kept, through the checks of the end of a session; 0 when all of it is taken and the view removed, 1 when anything is refused or left. \-\-list, given alone, prints the roles of the pinned revision that start starts, one to a line and nothing else. With \-v (\-\-verbose) each line adds whether the role runs in a view or in the whole tree, and whether the organisation's file clears a runner for it, for the planner a runner with start and planner up to the level of the repository. \-\-show\-origin prints as prompt does.
.PP
The session has a network of its own. It reaches only the endpoints of reach of the runner in the organisation's file, through a proxy that wb runs outside the sandbox, by CONNECT alone; HTTPS_PROXY and HTTP_PROXY name it. A runner with no reach reaches no network. The stamp has the line reach:, and each connection refused is a line of the file refused of the lines; after the session wb says how many were refused.
.PP
While the session runs, wb is its watchdog. Past quiet_minutes of the role with nothing changed in the view, it says quiet: on standard error and in the file quiet of the lines, and again each time the quiet doubles; the session goes on. Past max_minutes it ends the session as a termination of wb does, kills it after 10 seconds, takes its commits back, writes limit: max_minutes in outcome, and ends with 1. The limits are the rule table's, 30 and 240 minutes, or lower ones that session_limits of watchbill.yml sets; a higher one stops the start with 1 before anything is posted.
.PP
Ends with the session: 0 when it ended with 0, else 1; 1 when it is refused, 2 when it was used wrongly or the repository is not set up.
.PP
\-q (\-\-quiet) leaves out the size of a view of the whole tree and how to see what a commit changes, and not a refusal, what it says before it posts or writes, what is not pushed and the commands to push, or what \-\-prompt, \-\-stamp, \-\-parts, \-\-show\-origin and \-\-list print.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-runner <name>
The runner. The default is the organisation's.
.TP
.B \-\-prompt
Print as wb prompt does, and post and start nothing. So do \-\-stamp and \-\-parts.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-local
Start although a file the view reads has a local change: the session sees HEAD. A local change to a file under changes still stops the start.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-kept
Start although a view of the issue is kept with work not taken back. It passes that check alone, and the stamp says kept: ignored <n>.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-branch
Start although the checkout is on another branch than the branch of the stamp, at the same commit, or, where that branch is not there yet, on a branch that is not the default branch. It passes that check alone: the commits of the session still come back onto the branch of the stamp, and the branch checked out is not moved.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-push
Push nothing and open no change after the session: say the command that pushes the branch and, after a build, the command that opens the change, and run neither.
.TP
.B \-\-take\-back
Take back the kept view of the role and the issue. It is used with the role and the issue alone. The commits are taken as at the end of a session, onto the branch of the view's stamp, whichever branch is checked out: its HEAD is the commit the view was made from or a descendant of it whose changes touch none of the paths of the commits, and where it is not there it is made at the commit the view was made from. Where that branch is not checked out, only its ref is moved. Where the view still holds work not taken, it is kept and named. Before it takes, it reads every object the commits add and counts the commits, each git run within 3 seconds; during the take each git run ends within 60 seconds and the whole take\-back within 300 seconds. It says every commit, or the first 200 and and <k> more, and takes exactly that many. Where the lines of the take\-back cannot be written it ends with 1.
.TP
.B \-\-issue <text>
The issue, for {issue}. It is also the word after the role.
.TP
.B \-\-change <text>
The change, for {change}.
.TP
.B \-\-requirements <text>
The requirements, for {requirements}.
.TP
.B \-\-round <text>
The round, for {round}.
.TP
.B \-\-checkout <text>
The checkout, for {checkout}.
.TP
.B \-\-branch <text>
The branch, for {branch}.
.TP
.B \-\-issue_address <text>
The address of the issue, for {issue_address}.
.TP
.B \-\-change_address <text>
The address of the change, for {change_address}.
.TP
.B \-\-classed <text>
The classed paths, for {classed}.
.TP
.B \-\-classed_touched <text>
The classed paths the change touches, for {classed_touched}.
.TP
.B \-\-description <text>
The file of the change's description, for {description}. Only with \-\-prompt, \-\-stamp or \-\-parts: a session that wb starts writes it where wb names it, /run/watchbill/description in the sandbox.
.TP
.B \-\-brought <text>, \-\-questions <text>, \-\-change_comment <text>, \-\-needs_person <text>
The folder of what wb brings in, and the files a session writes for wb to post, for {brought}, {questions}, {change_comment} and {needs_person}. Only with \-\-prompt, \-\-stamp or \-\-parts: wb names them, /run/watchbill/in, /run/watchbill/questions, /run/watchbill/change_comment and /run/watchbill/needs_person in the sandbox.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the roles of the pinned revision that wb start starts, one to a line and nothing else. It is given alone, with \-v or \-\-verbose, and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
With \-\-list, add to each line whether the role runs in a view or in the whole tree, and whether the organisation's file clears a runner for it (cleared or not\-cleared).
.TP
.B \-\-show\-origin
Print the whole prompt with a line before each part that says where it comes from, as wb prompt does, and post and start nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-parts
Print the path and the SHA\-256 of each part, in the form sha256sum prints, in place of the text.
.TP
.B \-\-stamp
Print, in place of the text, what the assignment is: the role, its model and effort, the runner, the version and revision, the issue, the change, the round, the branch, the checkout, the time, and each part as \-\-parts gives it. Not with \-\-parts.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Leave out the lines that only confirm what was done or say there was nothing to do; never a refusal, a warning, what is said before a write, or a command the person has to run.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The session ended with 0.
.TP
.B 1
It is refused, or the session ended with anything else.
.TP
.B 2
It was used wrongly or the repository is not set up.
